  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Rain continues through this morning morning. \nSome drought relief is possible across Northern Maine. \nTemperatures this afternoon and Thursday could approach daily \nrecord high temperatures.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nWith a stationary/slow moving warm front currently positioned\nnear the Maine Coast, unsettled weather continues over the \nforecast area into this morning. Aloft, a 700 mb vort max \npersists over the Crown of Maine, persisting stratiform rain \nover the area. Through the morning, northern Maine may see an \nadditional half an inch of rainfall or so. For the Downeast \nregion, as the warm front gradually lifts northwards, the dry \nwarm sector will replace this synoptic feature, and \nprecipitation will clear the area.\n\nAs the warm sector takes over the entirety of northern and\neastern Maine this afternoon into the day on Thursday, southwest\nwarm air advection will allow for temperatures to warm, lifting\nwell above average and potentially even making a run for daily\nrecord high temperatures. Today highs may lift into the mid to\nupper 60s Downeast, while the north remains more modest due to\nthe lingering rain and only reaches into the upper 50s to around\n60. For Thursday, highs throughout the CWA could make a run for\nthe mid to upper 60s, and 70 will definitely be within reach,\nparticularly in the Bangor area.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Additional rainfall expected late Thursday into\nthe weekend, though exact timing and amounts are still \nuncertain. The rainfall could provide some drought relief.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nA large occlusion should move NE across Quebec and into the\nMaritimes Thursday through Friday. As the system progresses, a\nvertically stacked closed low should develop over the Great\nLakes. The large cold front from the occluded low will pull\nample moisture from the tropical southeast. On Friday morning,\nthe cold front should be across the Gulf Of Maine, slowly moving\nnorthward. Rain is expected to begin Downeast Friday afternoon\nand continue into Saturday. The second low over the Great Lakes\nmerges with the cold front, creating an intensifying low over\nthe Mid-Atlantic states. Expect an increase in rain showers\nDowneast from this. The new low is expected to move across the\nstate this weekend, bringing rain to the entire region. However,\nthe timing of the system is still uncertain. Thus total rainfall\namounts are still unknown.",
